    "analysis": "A laddered bond portfolio is constructed by distributing maturities across years to minimize concentration risk. Given a 5-year horizon, we'll allocate bond maturities in a 30%, 30%, 20%, 10%, and 10% structure. This ensures that no more than 30% of the portfolio matures in any single year, adhering to the laddered maturity constraint. To achieve this, we will recommend buying investment-grade bonds with 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6-year maturities. A suitable investment-grade bond for each year will be selected, with a focus on minimizing the portfolio duration while maintaining a credit rating of BBB+ or above. In a normal market, we'd expect a mix of government and corporate bonds to provide a stable return. However, due to the constraints, we'll prioritize investment-grade corporate bonds to achieve the desired maturity distribution.",
